------------------------\nThe conjecture that all finite-index subgroups of Thompson group F are 2-generated | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/333757/the-conjecture-that-all-finite-index-subgroups-of-thompson-g)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # The conjecture that all finite-index subgroups of Thompson group F are 2-generated ... Every finite-index subgroup of $F$F is $2$2-generated. ... Gili Golan, “The generation problem in Thompson group F”, arXiv:1608.02572 (2021).\n\n# The conjecture that all finite-index subgroups of Thompson group F are 2-generated\n\nAbout 10 years old · traced to 2016\n\nLet $F$F denote Thompson group $F$F, and let a group be 2-generated if it has a generating set with two elements. The finite-index generation conjecture. Every finite-index subgroup of $F$F is $2$2-generated. Finite-index subgroups of $F$F can have generating sets of this size in known examples, but the assertion for all finite-index subgroups is presented as a conjecture and remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nGili Golan, “The generation problem in Thompson group F”, arXiv:1608.02572 (2021).\n\nfinite-index-subgroupsgeometric-group-theorythompson-group-f\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProceedings of the Edinburgh Mathematical Society (2022) 65, 214–228 (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/40BCBAEFAE6B73A81A212E4D5B76BEE3/S0013091522000037a.pdf/on-the-spread-of-infinite-groups.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.6 years ago; Crawled: 4.5 years ago; Abstract  A group is 3/2-generated if every non-trivial element is part of a generating pair. In 2019, Donovan and Harper showed that many Thompson groups are 3/2-generated and posed five questions. ... The groups we consider are a family of finite index subgroups G1, G2, . . . of the Houghton group FSym(Z) × Z. ... Keywords: 3/2 generation; spread; infinite spread; infinite groups; Houghton groups; permutation groups\nProceedings of the Edinburgh Mathematical Society (2022) 65, 214–228  \ndoi:10.1017/S0013091522000037\n\nON THE SPREAD OF INFINITE GROUPS\n\nCHARLES GARNET COX\n\nSchool of Mathematics, University of Bristol, Bristol BS8 1UG, UK  \n(charles.cox@bristol.ac.uk)\n\n(Received 01 September 2021; first published online 8 February 2022)\n\nAbstract  A group is 3/2-generated if every non-trivial element is part of a generating pair. In 2019, Donovan and Harper showed that many Thompson groups are 3/2-generated and posed five questions. The first of these is whether there exists a 2-generated group with every proper quotient cyclic that is not 3/2-generated. This is a natural question given the significant work in proving that no finite group has this property, but we show that there is such an infinite group. The groups we consider are a family of finite index subgroups G1, G2, . . . of the Houghton group FSym(Z) × Z. We then show that G1 and G2 are 3/2-generated and investigate the related notion of spread for these groups. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCox, C. G. (2022). On the finite index subgroups of Houghton's (https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/310480097/Cox2022_Article_OnTheFiniteIndexSubgroupsOfHou.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; On the ﬁnite index subgroups of Houghton’s groups ... to be 2-generated for each c ∈N, by investigating the generation properties of ... Every ﬁnite index subgroup of a ﬁnitely generated group is itself ﬁnitely\nOn the ﬁnite index subgroups of Houghton’s groups\nWe then extend the work in [7], where the groups ⟨Alt(X2), gc\n2⟩were shown\nto be 2-generated for each c ∈N, by investigating the generation properties of\neach of these groups.\nNotation. For a ﬁnitely generated group G, let d(G) := min{|S| : ⟨S⟩= G}.\nEvery ﬁnite index subgroup of a ﬁnitely generated group is itself ﬁnitely\ngenerated. In the case of Zn, where n ∈N, we have that each A ≤f Zn satisﬁes\nd(A) = d(Zn). There has been much work related to this notion, e.g. [12] and\npapers verifying the Nielsen–Schreier formula for families of groups other than\nfree groups. Our second theorem states that there is a close connection between\nthe minimal number of generators of a Houghton group and its ﬁnite index\nsubgroups. Note that d(H2) = 2 and d(Hn) = n −1 for n ∈{3, 4, . . .}.\nTheorem 3. If U ≤f H2, then d(U) = d(H2). For n ∈{3, 4 . . .} and U ≤f Hn,\nwe have that d(U)\n∈\n{d(Hn), d(Hn) + 1}. Furthermore, let π(U)\n=\n⟨c2e1, . . . , cnen−1⟩. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAsian-European Journal of Mathematics (https://www.math.uaic.ro/~martar/pdf/articole/articol%2090.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; In this short note, we give a characterization of ZM-groups that uses the functions deﬁned ... Keywords: Gauss formula; Euler’s totient function; ﬁnite group; order of an element;\nAsian-European Journal of Mathematics\nVol. 11, No. 4 (2018) 1891001 (3 pages)\nc⃝World Scientiﬁc Publishing Company\nDOI: 10.1142/S1793557118910011\nAddendum\nOn a generalization of the Gauss formula\n[Asian-Eur. J. Math., Vol. 10, No. 1 (2017), Article ID 1750008,\nDOI: 10.1142/S1793557117500085]\nMarius T˘arn˘auceanu\nFaculty of Mathematics\n“Al.I. Cuza” University, Ia¸si, Romania\ntarnauc@uaic.ro\nReceived April 5, 2017\nRevised March 30, 2018\nAccepted April 3, 2018\nPublished May 11, 2018\nCommunicated by N. Gilbert\nIn this short note, we give a characterization of ZM-groups that uses the functions deﬁned\nand studied in [M. T˘arn˘auceanu, A generalization of the Euler’s totient function, Asian-\nEur. J. Math. 8(4) (2015) 1550087; M. T˘arn˘auceanu, On a generalization of the Gauss\nformula, Asian-Eur. J. Math. 10(1) (2017) 1750008]. This leads to a proof of Conjecture\n6 in [M. T˘arn˘auceanu, On a generalization of the Gauss formula, Asian-Eur. J. Math.\n10(1) (2017) 1750008].\nKeywords: Gauss formula; Euler’s totient function; ﬁnite group; order of an element;\nexponent of a group.\nAsian-European J. Math. 2018.11. Downloaded from www.worldscientific.com\nAMS Subject Classiﬁcation: 20D60, 11A25, 20D99, 11A99\n1. Introduction\nby 82.77.235.241 on 08/02/18. Re-use and distribution is strictly not permitted, except for Open Access articles.\nThe Euler’s totient function (or, simply, the totient function) ϕ is one of the most\nfamous functions in number theory. The totient ϕ(n) of a positive integer n is\ndeﬁned to be the number of positive integers less than or equal to n, that are\ncoprime to n. Note that there exist a lot of identities involving the totient function.\nOne of them is the Gauss formula\n\u0001\nd|n\nϕ(d) = n,\n∀n ∈N∗.\n(1)\nIn the last years, there has been a growing interest in extending arithmetical\nnotions to ﬁnite groups (see e.g. [1–4, 6, 9]). Following this trend, a group theoretical\n1891001-1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\"Polyhedral Problems in Combinatorial Convex Geometry\" by Liam Solus (https://uknowledge.uky.edu/math_etds/32/)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago;   * Liam Solus, University of Kentucky Follow ... We show that a well-studied unimodular triangulation of the (n,k)-hypersimplex restricts to a triangulation of each r-stable (n,k)-hypersimplex within. ... From this, we then extract some results on unimodality.\nThese are a family of polytopes which form a nested chain of subpolytopes within the (n,k)-hypersimplex. We show that a well-studied unimodular triangulation of the (n,k)-hypersimplex restricts to a triangulation of each r-stable (n,k)-hypersimplex within. We then use this triangulation to compute the facet-defining inequalities of these polytopes. In the k=2 case, we use shelling techniques to devise a combinatorial interpretation of the coefficients of the h*-polynomials in terms of independent sets of certain graphs. From this, we then extract some results on unimodality. We also characterize the Gorenstein r-stable (n,k)-hypersimplices, and we conclude that these also have unimodal h*-polynomials.\n\nIn the second case, for a graph G on p vertices we consider the closure of the cone of concentration matrices of G. The extreme rays of this cone, and their associated ranks, have applications in maximum likelihood estimation for the undirected Gaussian graphical model associated to G. Consequently, the extreme ranks of this cone have been well-studied. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA SHELLING OF THE ODD SECOND HYPERSIMPLEX (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=d498aa25bf3a43e223015f3e425c5f1e05df4e37&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; unimodality. ... that we call the r-stable hypersimplex. ... regular unimodular triangulation of the hypersimplex induces a regular unimodular triangulation\n2\nBENJAMIN BRAUN AND LIAM SOLUS\nvol(P) =\nP\ni h∗\ni\nd!\n, where vol(P) denotes the Euclidean Volume (Lebesgue measure) of P with respect\nto the integer lattice contained in the hyperplane spanned by P.\nWe call the sum P\ni h∗\ni the\nnormalized volume of P. Another useful (but not always achieved) property of these vectors is\nunimodality. A vector (x0, x1, . . . , xd) is called unimodal if there exists an index j, 0 ≤j ≤d, such\nthat xi−1 ≤xi for i ≤j, and xi ≥xi+1 for i ≥j. Unimodality of the h∗-vector has interesting\nalgebraic implications and consequently is a widely sought property of these vectors [4, 5, 9]. In\nthe special case of the hypersimplex, Haws, De Loera, and K¨oppe computationally veriﬁed that\nthe h∗-vector of ∆n,k is unimodal for n as large as 75 [1].\nSince the h∗-vector of ∆n,k is not\nsymmetric, these results are intriguing from both the perspective of investigation of the h∗-vectors\nof hypersimplices as well as the perspective of investigation of unimodal sequences.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTitle of your research plan: (https://www.jsps.go.jp/file/storage/general/english/e-summer/data/2014/All_Research_Report.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.0 years ago; Unimodality of the h*-polynomial of the r-stable n,k-hypersimplex.\nTitle of your research plan:\n\nUnimodality of the h*-polynomial of the r-stable n,k-hypersimplex.\n\nDescription of the research activities: We call these subpolytopes nested within the hypersimplex the r-stable hypersimplices. The hypersimplex is the special case when r=1. A polynomial of degree d in the variable x is called symmetric whenever the coefficients of x_i and x_{d-i} are equal for every i=0,1,...,d. If an r-stable hypersimplex has a symmetric h*-polynomial then, since it has a regular unimodular triangulation, it is also unimodal. Hence, this Summer we identified the collection of r-stable hypersimplices with symmetric h*-polynomials. These polytopes are called the Gorenstein r-stable hypersimplices. To do this, we computed the equations of the “facets” (the highest dimensional (nontrivial) faces) of the r-stable hypersimplices. Using these equations and a well-known technique developed by the Host Researcher we determined the Gorenstein r-stable hypersimplices. The Gorenstein property is quite rare amongst the r-stable hypersimplices. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) A characterization of ZM-groups (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/316035264_A_characterization_of_ZM-grou